From naomi at stat.psu.edu  Fri Jul 24 13:40:17 2009
From: naomi at stat.psu.edu (Naomi Altman)
Date: Fri, 24 Jul 2009 07:40:17 -0400
Subject: [BioC] Statistics for next-generation sequencing transcriptomics
In-Reply-To: <8D08960C647E64438CE5740657CBBDC5F8E98A8D@iahcexch1.iah.bbs
	rc.ac.uk>
References: <8D08960C647E64438CE5740657CBBDC5F8E98A8D@iahcexch1.iah.bbsrc.ac.uk>
Message-ID: <6.2.3.4.2.20090724073711.0258dc60@imap.stat.psu.edu>

The problem is the FDR vs FNR problem (which we used to call power vs 
size).  As the total sample size increases, we have the power to 
detect tiny, biologically negligible, differences.  All frequentist 
tests will suffer from this, not just Fisher's exact test.

--Naomi

At 07:22 AM 7/24/2009, michael watson (IAH-C) wrote:
